Adopting this stance requires initiating one-on-one conversations before proposing any group structure. It involves testing written communication tools to reduce verbal ambiguity while monitoring whether this reduces defensiveness. There is a commitment to evaluate the emotional cost incurred by the user during these repair attempts, with a trigger to seek neutral mediation if facilitation becomes burdensome. It accepts the possibility that defining quality standards collaboratively may not resolve underlying resentment regarding fairness. This view holds that schedule volatility must be accommodated rather than resisted, but acknowledges this may not suffice if the avoidance stems from incompatibility rather than overload. Finally, it commits to treating the failure of re-engagement as data regarding the living arrangement's viability, rather than merely a behavioral problem to be solved.